  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 258 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGetting over the hump into late next week, we find ourselves \nunder a weak ridge between two longwave troughs one to the east \nover the Great Lakes and the other moving onshore over \nCalifornia. The ridge will be brief and bring high pressure with\nsubsidence at the surface which should help temperatures \nrecover into the 60s on Friday. Speaking of Friday, the \naforementioned trough over the western US becomes relevant for \nour area. Guidance projects it to take more of a northeast track\nalong the jetstream. An upper-level jet with southwest winds \naloft is to come from this at the 300-500mb levels. Down at the \nsurface, a low-pressure system and cold front forms and \ninfluences our coverage area. This brings to light our next \nlegit chance of precipitation. After this system passes north of\nus late Saturday, winds become zonal as another modest ridge \npropagates towards the county Warning Area (CWA). Winds aloft \nthen shift back to the southwest as another trough looms to the \nwest.\n\nAs mentioned above, our first shot at precip is in store late \nFriday into Saturday morning. With a low off to the \nnorth/northeast and an associated cold front. It is expected to \nbe wet across the CWA with dewpoints in the 40s/low 50 so ample \nmoisture for sure. Despite everything in place for a decent rain\nevent, guidance is still not very confident on a good steady \nrain. PoPs in the 30-50% range is forecasted so at least some \nrain should occur. Any rain that does fall shouldn't accumulate \nto much as QPF and other accumulations estimate in the range of \n0.01 to 0.05. Therefore, there is still low confidence we get \nanything more than light rain showers. Trends will be monitored \nwith this as the week goes on.\n\nHigh temperatures will be quite nice for this time of the year.\nAbove average highs are likely across the board through Friday.\nEnsembles agree quite well on seasonably warm highs with upper \n50/60s expected. There's a slight chance (30-40%) that it \nreaches 70 degrees for parts of the CWA on Friday before that \nthe front drops temperatures quite a bit. Saturday and Sunday's \npost-frontal temps should be much more normal for this time of \nthe year, sitting around 50 to 55 degrees. Overnight lows should\nvary quite a bit from west to east on Friday as the slow moving\ncold front advances eastward. Morning lows in the 40s are \nanticipated east of Highway 27 and mid to upper 30s west of \nthere. Post-front, lows in 30s across the entire CWA is in play.\nAfter that, radiational cooling should lead to our coldest \nnight of the season so far going into Sunday with lows expected \nin the 20s pretty much everywhere. Some locations may get as \ncold as 20 degrees on Sunday morning.\n\nFire weather remains a non-factor across the Tri-State area. \nRelative Humidity (RH) should be in the 40-70% range for the \nentire forecast period as a slew of relatively wet air masses \ndrift through the region. RHs should not fall below 30% at all \nbut if they do, it would be in a few spots throughout eastern \nColorado. The driest day will be Saturday but again, not \nconcerned at all with GFDI values well below fire weather \ncriteria. It will likely be breezy through the weekend with \nsurface winds consistently from the south at around 20-30 mph. \nFriday should be the windiest day with sustained winds up to 40 \nmph and gusting up to 55 mph. Winds then die down quite a bit \ngoing into next weekend as just 5-20 mph is forecast through \nSunday.",
